lesson physics-brighter-and-dimmer v=1 format=teach grade=4 unit=4 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Brighter and dimmer"
  goal "Predict how bright the bulbs in a circuit will be from the number of cells and bulbs, and choose the change that makes them brighter."

  look {
    scene 35x27 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 0) space=1 align=start {
        row top space=2 align=start {
          series one cells=1
          series more cells=2
        }
        row bottom space=2 align=start {
          series two cells=1 bulbs=2
          note n "Two cells push harder, so the bulb is brighter. Two bulbs share one cell, so each is dimmer." width=16
        }
      }
    }
    say """
      The cells push the electricity round, and the bulbs share that push. Add a cell and each bulb
      gets more, so it is brighter. Add a bulb and each one gets less, so they are all dimmer. Two
      cells with two bulbs gives each bulb the same as one cell with one bulb.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice physics.brighter-of-two count=3 seed=6
    practice physics.brighter-change count=2 seed=4
    practice physics.dimmer-change count=2 seed=5
  }
  story {
    say "The cells are not the only way to push electricity round. A wind turbine turns the push of moving air into electricity, and a cable carries it to the lamps."
    practice physics.turbine-lamps count=2 seed=1
    show physics.energy-chain
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show physics.add-both c=1 n=2 easy-n=1 hard-c=2 hard-n=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show physics.series-count n=3 easy-n=2
  }
  remember {
    say "More cells, brighter bulbs. More bulbs, dimmer bulbs."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Children often expect the first bulb in a line to be brighter than the second, as though the
    electricity were used up on the way round. It is not: in one loop, identical bulbs are equally
    bright, which is worth building with two bulbs and seeing. If a bulb is rated for one or two
    cells, do not add a fourth, or it will blow. The turbine questions follow the energy from the
    wind to the lamp; a toy windmill and a torch make the same point, that energy is passed along
    and not made from nothing.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the two circuits differ plainly in cells for each bulb, the wind is light, and the added cell and bulb leave the bulbs the same."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the two circuits are close or equal in cells for each bulb, the wind is strong, and the added cell and bulb make the bulbs dimmer."
  }
}
